efore, R/r \u2248 [a^(3/2)/(8 sqrt(\u03b5)) ] / [ (1/2) sqrt(\u03b5 a) ] = [a^(3/2)/(8 sqrt(\u03b5)) ] * [2 / (sqrt(\u03b5 a)) ] = [2 a^(3/2) ] / [8 sqrt(\u03b5) * sqrt(\u03b5 a) ] = [2 a^(3/2) ] / [8 \u03b5 sqrt(a) ] = [2 a^(3/2) ] / [8 \u03b5 a^(1/2) ] = [2 a] / [8 \u03b5] = a/(4 \u03b5). \n\nMeanwhile, altitude h = 2\u0394/a \u2248 2*(a/2) sqrt(\u03b5 a)/a = sqrt(\u03b5 a). \n\nThus, a/h = a / sqrt(\u03b5 a) = sqrt(a)/sqrt(\u03b5). \n\nTherefore, R/r \u2248 a/(4 \u03b5), and a/h \u2248 sqrt(a)/sqrt(\u03b5). \n\nIf we fix a =1, then R/r \u2248 1/(4 \u03b5), and a/h \u2248 1/sqrt(\u03b5). \n\nSo as \u03b5 approaches 0, R/r ~ 1/(4 \u03b5) and a/h ~ 1/sqrt(\u03b5). Therefore, for sufficiently small \u03b5, 1/(4 \u03b5) > 1/sqrt(\u03b5) is equivalent to 1/(4) > sqrt(\u03b5), which is true as long as \u03b5 < 1/16. So as \u03b5 approaches zero, initially R/r is greater than a/h, but once \u03b5 becomes less than 1/16, then R/r becomes less than a/h. Wait, that contradicts our numerical example where with \u03b5 = 0.0001 (which is less than 1/16 \u2248 0.0625), R/r was still greater. \n\nWait, maybe my approximations are not accurate for such small \u03b5. Let's compute for \u03b5 = 0.0001, so sqrt(\u03b5) = 0.01. Then R/r \u2248 1/(4 * 0.0001) = 2500. a/h \u2248 1 / 0.01 = 100. So indeed, 2500 > 100. Wait, but according to the previous analysis, when \u03b5 < 1/16, then 1/(4 \u03b5) > 1/sqrt(\u03b5) would require sqrt(\u03b5) < 1/4, which is equivalent to \u03b5 < 1/16. So when \u03b5 < 1/16, 1/(4 \u03b5) is greater than 1/sqrt(\u03b5). For example, \u03b5 = 0.01: 1/(4*0.01) = 25, 1/sqrt(0.01)=10, 25>10. For \u03b5 =0.0001: 1/(4*0.0001)=2500, 1/sqrt(0.0001)=100, 2500>100. So actually, for all \u03b5 >0, 1/(4 \u03b5) > 1/sqrt(\u03b5) is equivalent to 1/4 > sqrt(\u03b5), which is true for \u03b5 < 1/16. Wait, no. Wait, solving 1/(4 \u03b5) > 1/sqrt(\u03b5) multiply both sides by 4 \u03b5 sqrt(\u03b5) (positive), we get sqrt(\u03b5) > 4 \u03b5. Which is sqrt(\u03b5) -4 \u