k to b c > 2 r a. \n\nAlternatively, use trigonometric identities. Since r = 4 R sin(A/2) sin(B/2) sin(C/2), and we need to show R/r > a/h. Let's express a/h in terms of angles. \n\nSince a = 2 R sin A, h = 2\u0394/a = 2*(1/2 a b sin C)/a = b sin C. So h = b sin C. But since a is the longest side, angle A is the largest angle, so angle A \u2265 angle B, angle C. Also, in any triangle, h must be the altitude corresponding to the longest side a, hence h = b sin C = c sin B. \n\nSo a/h = (2 R sin A)/(b sin C). But b = 2 R sin B. Therefore, a/h = (2 R sin A)/(2 R sin B sin C) ) = sin A/(sin B sin C). \n\nSimilarly, R/r = 1/(4 sin(A/2) sin(B/2) sin(C/2)). Therefore, the inequality becomes:\n\n1/(4 sin(A/2) sin(B/2) sin(C/2)) > sin A/(sin B sin C)\n\nMultiply both sides by 4 sin(A/2) sin(B/2) sin(C/2) sin B sin C:\n\nsin B sin C > 4 sin(A/2) sin(B/2) sin(C/2) sin A\n\nSimplify the right-hand side. Note that sin A = 2 sin(A/2) cos(A/2). So RHS becomes 4 sin(A/2) sin(B/2) sin(C/2) * 2 sin(A/2) cos(A/2) = 8 sin\u00b2(A/2) sin(B/2) sin(C/2) cos(A/2). \n\nBut this seems complicated. Let me look for another identity. Recall that in a triangle:\n\nsin A + sin B + sin C = 4 cos(A/2) cos(B/2) cos(C/2)\n\nBut not sure if that helps. Alternatively, use product-to-sum formulas. \n\nAlternatively, consider that in any triangle:\n\nsin B sin C = [cos(B - C) - cos(B + C)]/2 = [cos(B - C) + cos A]/2, since B + C = \u03c0 - A, so cos(B + C) = -cos A. \n\nTherefore, sin B sin C = [cos(B - C) + cos A]/2. \n\nSimilarly, sin A = 2 sin(A/2) cos(A/2). \n\nTherefore, the inequality sin B sin C > 8 sin\u00b2(A/2) sin(B/2) sin(C/2) cos(A/2). \n\nThis seems messy. Maybe another approach. Let me consider substituting angles. Let\u2019s denote x = A/2, y = B/2, z = C/2. Then x + y + z = \u03c0/2. The inequality becomes:\n\n1/(4 sin x sin y sin z) > sin(2x)/(sin 2y sin 2z)\n\nMultiply both sides by 4 sin x sin y sin z sin 2y sin 2z:\n\nsin 2y sin 2z > 4 sin x sin y sin z sin 2x\n\nBut sin 2y = 2 sin y