} = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\)\n\nThen \\( s_1^2 + s_1 = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} + \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} = \\frac{4 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{2} = 2 + \\sqrt{5} \\)\n\nAlternatively, if we compute \\( s_1^3 \\), let's see:\n\n\\( s_1^3 = s_1 \\cdot s_1^2 = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} = \\frac{(1)(3) + (1)(\\sqrt{5}) + 3(\\sqrt{5}) + (\\sqrt{5})^2}{4} = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5} + 3\\sqrt{5} + 5}{4} = \\frac{8 + 4\\sqrt{5}}{4} = 2 + \\sqrt{5} \\). Same result. So, \\( p = 2 + \\sqrt{5} \\).\n\nSimilarly, for \\( s_2 = \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\):\n\nCompute \\( s_2^2 \\):\n\\( s_2^2 = \\left( \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{1 - 2\\sqrt{5} + 5}{4} = \\frac{6 - 2\\sqrt{5}}{4} = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\)\n\nThen \\( s_2^2 + s_2 = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} + \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} = \\frac{4 - 2\\sqrt{5}}{2} = 2 - \\sqrt{5} \\)\n\nAlternatively, compute \\( s_2^3 \\):\n\\( s_2^3 = s_2 \\cdot s_2^2 = \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} = \\frac{(1)(3) + (1)(- \\sqrt{5}) + (- \\sqrt{5})(3) + (- \\sqrt{5})^2}{4} = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{5} - 3\\sqrt{5} + 5}{4} = \\frac{8 - 4\\sqrt{5}}{4} = 2 - \\sqrt{5} \\). So, \\( p = 2 - \\sqrt{5} \\).\n\nTherefore, the possible values of \\( p \\) are \\( 2 + \\sqrt{5} \\) and \\( 2 - \\sqrt{5} \\). \n\nBut wait, the problem states that the roots are distinct. Let's check if these values of \\( p \\) lead to distinct roots. \n\nFor \\( p = 2 + \\sqrt{5} \\), the roots are \\( s = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\) and \\( r = s^2 = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\). Since \\( \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\approx 1.618 \\) and \\( \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\approx 2.618 \\), these are distinct.\n\nFor \\( p = 2 - \\sqrt{5} \\), the roots are \\( s = \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\approx -0.618 \\) and \\( r = s^2 = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\approx 0.381 \\). These are also distinct. \n\nTherefore, both values of \\( p \\) satisfy the condition of distinct roots. \n\nWait, but let's verify by plugging ba