t) = \\ln t + \\frac{4}{t+1} - 2 \\text{ is increasing since } p^{\\prime}(t) > 0.\n     \\]\n     Then,\n     \\[\n     p(1) = 0 \\Rightarrow \\ln t > \\frac{2(t-1)}{t+1}.\n     \\]\n   - To prove \\( \\frac{t-1}{\\ln t} < \\frac{t+1}{2} \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{2(t-1)}{t+1} < \\ln t < \\frac{t - \\frac{1}{t}}{2}.\n     \\]\n     Define a second function:\n     \\[\n     q(t) = \\ln t - \\frac{1}{2}(t - \\frac{1}{t}).\n     \\]\n     To compare:\n     \\[\n     q^{\\prime}(t) = \\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\ln t - \\frac{1}{2}(t - \\frac{1}{t})\\right)\n     = \\frac{1}{t} - \\frac{1}{2}\\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{t^2}\\right)\n     = \\frac{-t^2 + 2t - 1}{2t^2}\n     = - \\frac{(t-1)^2}{2t^2} < 0.\n     \\]\n     The function \\( q(t) \\) is decreasing and:\n     \\[\n     q(1) = 0 \\Rightarrow \\ln t < \\sqrt{t} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\n     \\]\n   \n5. **Conclusion**: Combining the two parts, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{a b} < \\frac{a - b}{\\ln a - \\ln b} < \\frac{a + b}{2}.\n   \\]\n   \n   Thus, we have\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Part 1 is proved: } \\sqrt{ab} < \\frac{a-b}{\\ln a - \\ln b} < \\frac{a + b}{2}}.\n   \\]\n\n#### Part 2:\n\n1. **Given**: \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are zero points of \\( f(x) = \\ln x - \\frac{1}{2017}x \\).\n\n2. **From the zero points**:\n   \\[\n   \\ln a = \\frac{1}{2017}a \\quad \\text{ and } \\quad \\ln b = \\frac{1}{2017}b.\n   \\]\n   Adding these equations:\n   \\[\n   \\ln a + \\ln b = \\frac{1}{2017}(a + b).\n   \\]\n   That is, \n   \\[\n   \\ln (ab) = \\frac{1}{2017}(a + b).\n   \\]\n   \n3. **Applying inequality**:\n   Given:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a+b}{\\ln (ab)} = 2017.\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\ln(ab) > 2 \\Rightarrow ab > e^2}.\n   \\]\n   \n   Hence, the solution to the second part is proved, and we conclude:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{ab > e^2}.\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Is there a triangle in which the shortest median is longer than the longest angle bisector?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the question is asking if there's a triangle where the shortest median is longer than the longest angle bisector. Hmm, interesting. Let me start by recalling what medians and angle bisectors are in a triangle. \n\nFirst, a median of a triangle is a line segment joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposing side. Every triangle has three medians, and they all intersect at the centroid, which divides each median into a 2:1 ratio. The length of a median can be calculated using the formula: \n\nFor a triangle with sides a, b, c, the median from the side a is m_a = (1/2)\u221a(2b\u00b2 + 2c\u00b2 - a\u00b2). Similarly for m_b and m_c.\n\nAn angle bisector is a line that divides the angle into two equal parts. The length of the angle bisector can be found using the angle bisector theorem or the formula: \n\nFor angle bisector from angle A, the length is t_a = 2bc cos(A/2) / (b + c). There are similar formulas for t_b and t_c.\n\nNow, the question is whether there exists a triangle where the shortest median is longer than the longest angle bisector. Let's unpack this. We need to compare the medians and the angle bisectors. The shortest median would be the one corresponding to the longest side, since the median length is inversely related to the side length (as per the formula: longer the side, shorter the median?). Wait, actually, let me check. If we have a triangle with sides a, b, c, ordered such that a \u2264 b \u2264 c, then the median to side c (the longest side) would be m_c = (1/2)\u221a(2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 - c\u00b2). Similarly, the median to side a (the shortest side) would be m_a = (1/2)\u221a(2b\u00b2 + 2c\u00b2 - a\u00b2). So actually, the median to the shorter side is longer. So if the sides are ordered a \u2264 b \u2264 c, then the medians would satisfy m_a \u2265 m_b \u2265 m_c. Therefore, the shortest median is m_c, corresponding to the longest side c. Conversely, the longest median is m_a, 