e angle bisector from the largest angle (gamma) is equal to the median to the longest side. Both are 4. So in this case, they are equal, but not the shortest median exceeding the longest angle bisector.\n\nThis suggests that in some cases, the angle bisector from the largest angle can be equal to the median to the longest side, but generally, the longest angle bisector is longer than the shortest median.\n\nAfter all these examples, it appears that the shortest median is always shorter than or equal to the longest angle bisector in any triangle. Therefore, the answer is no, such a triangle does not exist.\n\nBut to confirm, let's check if there's a mathematical proof of this.\n\nSuppose we want to show that in any triangle, the shortest median is not longer than the longest angle bisector.\n\nGiven a triangle with sides a \u2264 b \u2264 c. Then, the shortest median is m_c = (1/2)\u221a(2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 ). The longest angle bisector is t_\u03b1, from the smallest angle \u03b1 (opposite side a).\n\nWe need to show that m_c \u2264 t_\u03b1.\n\nUsing the formulas:\n\nm_c\u00b2 = (2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)/4\n\nt_\u03b1\u00b2 = [ bc( (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 ) ] / (b +c)^2\n\nWe need to prove that (2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)/4 \u2264 [ bc( (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 ) ] / (b +c)^2\n\nMultiply both sides by 4(b +c)^2:\n\n(2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)(b +c)^2 \u2264 4bc( (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 )\n\nThis is the same inequality as before. If this holds for all triangles, then the conclusion follows.\n\nAlternatively, consider the Ravi substitution. Let\u2019s set a = x + y, b = y + z, c = z + x, where x, y, z >0. But this might complicate things further.\n\nAlternatively, use trigonometric identities. Since angle \u03b1 is opposite side a, using the Law of Cosines:\n\ncos \u03b1 = (b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -a\u00b2)/(2bc)\n\nThen, t_\u03b1 = 2bc cos(\u03b1/2)/(b +c )\n\nWe need to compare m_c and t_\u03b1.\n\nExpress m_c in terms of angles. In triangle ABC, median m_c can be expressed using formula involving the sides.\n\nAlternatively, use the formula for the length of the median:\n\nm_c = (1/2)\u221a(2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 )\n\nAnd for the angle bisector:\n\nt_\u03b1 = 2bc cos(\u03b1/2)/(b +c )\n\nWe can use the half-angle formula:\n\ncos(\u03b1/2) = \u221a[ (1 + cos \u03b1)/2 ] = \u221a[ (1 + (b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -a\u00b2)/(2bc))/2 ] = \u221a[ (2bc +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -a\u00b2)/(4bc) ] = \u221a[ ( (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 )/(4bc) ]\n\nThus,\n\nt_\u03b1 = 2bc / (b +c ) * \u221a[ ( (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 )/(4bc) ] = \u221a[ ( (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 ) * bc ] / (b +c )\n\nSo t_\u03b1 = \u221a[ bc( (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 ) ] / (b +c )\n\nTherefore, t_\u03b1\u00b2 = bc( (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 ) / (b +c )\u00b2 = bc[ (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 ] / (b +c )\u00b2\n\nThus, the inequality m_c\u00b2 \u2264 t_\u03b1\u00b2 becomes:\n\n(2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)/4 \u2264 bc[ (b +c)^2 -a\u00b2 ] / (b +c )\u00b2\n\nMultiply both sides by 4(b +c )\u00b2:\n\n(2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)(b +c )\u00b2 \u2264 4bc[ (b +c )\u00b2 -a\u00b2 ]\n\nLet\u2019s denote S = b +c. Then, the inequality becomes:\n\n(2a\u00b2 + 2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2)S\u00b2 \u22644bc(S\u00b2 -a\u00b2 )\n\nExpand the left side:\n\n2a\u00b2S\u00b2 +2b\u00b2S\u00b2 -c\u00b2S\u00b2 \u22644bcS\u00b2 -4bca\u00b2\n\nRearrange all terms to the left:\n\n2a\u00b2S\u00b2 +2b\u00b2S\u00b2 -c\u00b2S\u00b2 -4bcS\u00b2 +4bca\u00b2 \u22640\n\nFactor terms:\n\nS\u00b2(2a\u00b2 +2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -4bc) +4bca\u00b2 \u22640\n\nHmm, this seems complicated. Let\u2019s plug S =b +c.\n\nThen, S\u00b2 =b\u00b2 +2bc +c\u00b2\n\nSo,\n\n(b\u00b2 +2bc +c\u00b2)(2a\u00b2 +2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -4bc) +4bca\u00b2 \u22640\n\nExpand the product:\n\n(b\u00b2)(2a\u00b2 +2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -4bc) +2bc(2a\u00b2 +2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -4bc) +c\u00b2(2a\u00b2 +2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -4bc) +4bca\u00b2 \u22640\n\nThis will take a while, but let's proceed step by step.\n\nFirst term: b\u00b2*(2a\u00b2 +2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -4bc)=2a\u00b2b\u00b2 +2b^4 -b\u00b2c\u00b2 -4b^3c\n\nSecond term:2bc*(2a\u00b2 +2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -4bc)=4a\u00b2bc +4b^3c -2bc^3 -8b^2c\u00b2\n\nThird term:c\u00b2*(2a\u00b2 +2b\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -4bc)=2a\u00b2c\u00b2 +2b\u00b2c\u00b2 -c^4 -4bc^3\n\nFourth term:+4bca\u00b2\n\nCombine all terms:\n\n2a\u00b2b\u00b2 +2b^4 -b\u00b2c\u00b2 -4b^3c +4a\u00b2bc +4b^3c -2bc^3 -8b\u00b2c\u00b2 +2a\u00b2c\u00b2 +2b\u00b2c\u00b2 -c^4 -4bc^3 +4a\u00b2bc\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n2a\u00b2b\u00b2 +2b^4 -b\u00b2c\u00b2 -4b^3c +4a\u00b2bc +4b^3c -2bc^3 -8b\u00b2c\u00b2 +2a\u00b2c\u00b2 +2b\u00b2c\u00b2 -c^4 -4bc^3 +4a\u00b2bc\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n- For a\u00b2b\u00b2: 2a\u00b2b\u00b2\n\n- For a\u00b2c\u00b2:2a\u00b2c\u00b2\n\n- For a\u00b2bc:4a\u00b2bc +4a\u00b2bc=8a\u00b2bc\n\n- For b^4:2b^4\n\n- For b^3c:-4b^3c +4b^3c=0\n\n- For b\u00b2c\u00b2:-b\u00b2c\u00b2 -8b\u00b2c\u00b2 +2b\u00b2c\u00b2=(-1 -8 +2)b\u00b2c\u00b2=-7b\u00b2c\u00b2\n\n- For bc^3:-2bc^3 -4bc^3=-6bc^3\n\n- For c^4:-c^4\n\nSo the entire expression becomes:\n\n2a\u00b2b\u00b2 +2a\u00b2c\u00b2 +8a\u00b2bc +2b^4 -7b\u00b2c\u00b2 -6bc\u00b3 -c^4 \u22640\n\nThis is the same inequality as before. Therefore, we need to check whether 2a\u00b2b\u00b2 +2a\u00b2c\u00b2 +8a\u00b2bc +2b^4 -7b\u00b2c\u00b2 -6bc\u00b3 -c^4 \u22640 for any triangle with sides a \u2264b \u2264c.\n\nBut how?\n\nAlternatively, factor the expression. Let\u2019s see:\n\n2a\u00b2b\u00b2 +2a\u00b2c\u00b2 +8a\u00b2bc +2b^4 -7b\u00b2c\u00b2 -6bc\u00b3 -c^4.\n\nPerhaps group terms:\n\n=2a\u00b2b\u00b2 +2a\u00b2c\u00b2 +8a\u00b2bc +2b^4 -7b\u00b2c\u00b2 -6bc\u00b3 -c^4\n\n=2a\u00b2(b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 +4bc) +2b^4 -7b\u00b2c\u00b2 -6bc\u00b3 -c^4.\n\nNote that b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 +4bc = (b +2c)^2 -3c\u00b2, but not sure if helpful.\n\nAlternatively, consider it as a quadratic in a\u00b2:\n\n2(b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 +4bc)a\u00b2 + (2b^4 -7b\u00b2c\u00b2 -6bc\u00b3 -c^4) \u22640\n\nBut since a\u00b2 must satisfy the triangle inequality, a >0, and a +b >c, etc.\n\nBut as a quadratic in a\u00b2, this would be of the form:\n\nPa\u0