ly marbles were drawn.\n\nHmm, this seems like a conditional expectation problem. So we need to compute E[X | no ugly marbles were drawn in all trials], where X is the number of trials until the first special marble.\n\nLet me recall that in probability, the expectation of a geometrically distributed random variable (which models the number of trials until the first success) is 1/p, where p is the probability of success. But here, we have a condition, so we need to adjust the probability accordingly.\n\nWait, but with the condition that no ugly marbles were drawn, each trial is effectively a Bernoulli trial where only blue and special marbles are possible. So perhaps the problem reduces to a geometric distribution where the probability of success is the probability of drawing the special marble given that the drawn marble is not ugly.\n\nSo the original probability of drawing the special marble is 1/20. The probability of drawing a blue marble is 9/20. The probability of drawing an ugly marble is 10/20 = 1/2.\n\nBut since we are given that no ugly marbles were drawn, the trials are only considering the cases where each draw is either blue or special. So we can think of this as a conditional probability scenario. The probability of success (drawing the special marble) given that the draw is not ugly.\n\nSo the conditional probability p would be (1/20) / (10/20) = 1/10? Wait, no. Wait, the probability that a draw is either blue or special is 9 + 1 = 10 marbles out of 20. So the probability of drawing a blue or special is 10/20 = 1/2. So the conditional probability of drawing the special marble, given that it's not ugly, is (1/20) / (10/20) = 1/10.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to a geometric distribution with p = 1/10. The expectation of such a geometric distribution is 1/p = 10. But wait, is that correct?\n\nWait, but hold on. The standard geometric distribution counts the number of trials until the first success, including the success. So if we have p = 1/10, th