:\nThe sum of the positive numbers \\(a, b, c\\), and \\(d\\) does not exceed 4. Find the maximum value of the expression:\n\\[ \n\\sqrt[4]{2 a^{2} + a^{2} b} + \\sqrt[4]{2 b^{2} + b^{2} c} + \\sqrt[4]{2 c^{2} + c^{2} d} + \\sqrt[4]{2 d^{2} + d^2 a} \n\\]\n\n\nFirst solution. Using the Power Mean Inequality for four numbers, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2 a^{2} + a^{2} b} = \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3 a \\cdot 3 a \\cdot (2 + b) \\cdot 3}}{\\sqrt[4]{27}} \\leqslant \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{27}} \\cdot \\frac{3 a + 3 a + (2 + b) + 3}{4} = \\frac{6 a + b + 5}{4 \\sqrt[4]{27}}\n\\]\n\nSumming this inequality with three similar inequalities, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\sqrt[4]{2 a^{2} + a^{2} b} + \\sqrt[4]{2 b^{2} + b^{2} c} + \\sqrt[4]{2 c^{2} + c^{2} d} + \\sqrt[4]{2 d^{2} + d^2 a} \\\\\n& \\leqslant \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{27}} \\left( \\frac{6 a + b + 5}{4} + \\frac{6 b + c + 5}{4} + \\frac{6 c + d + 5}{4} + \\frac{6 d + a + 5}{4} \\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{27}} \\cdot \\frac{7(a + b + c + d) + 20}{4} \\leqslant \\frac{12}{\\sqrt[4]{27}} = 4 \\sqrt[4]{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquality is achieved when \\(a = b = c = d = 1\\).\n\nSecond solution. Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for the sets of numbers \\(\\sqrt{a}, \\sqrt{b}, \\sqrt{c}, \\sqrt{d}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[4]{2+b}, \\sqrt[4]{2+c}, \\sqrt[4]{2+d}, \\sqrt[4]{2+a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left( \\sqrt[4]{2 a^{2} + a^{2} b} + \\sqrt[4]{2 b^{2} + b^{2} c} + \\sqrt[4]{2 c^{2} + c^{2} d} + \\sqrt[4]{2 d^{2} + d^2 a} \\right)^{2} & \\leqslant (a + b + c + d)(\\sqrt{2 + a} + \\sqrt{2 + b} + \\sqrt{2 + c} + \\sqrt{2 + d}) \\\\\n& \\leqslant 4(\\sqrt{2 + a} + \\sqrt{2 + b} + \\sqrt{2 + c} + \\sqrt{2 + d})\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n### Third solution (detailed):\n\nUsing the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for the sets of numbers \\(\\sqrt{a}, \\sqrt{b}, \\sqrt{c}, \\sqrt{d}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[4]{2+b}, \\sqrt[4]{2+c}, \\sqrt[4]{2+d}, \\sqrt[4]{2+a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\left( \\sqrt[4]{2 a^{2} + a^{2} b}