\nr + s = s - (s - 1)k + s = 2s - (s - 1)k\n\nThen, compute (s - r):\n\ns - r = s - [ s - (s - 1)k ] = (s - 1)k\n\nTherefore, left side becomes:\n\n[2s - (s - 1)k]^2 + h\u00b2[(s - 1)k]^2\n\nExpand:\n\n= 4s\u00b2 - 4s(s - 1)k + (s - 1)^2 k\u00b2 + h\u00b2(s - 1)^2 k\u00b2\n\nFactor out (s - 1)^2 k\u00b2:\n\n= 4s\u00b2 - 4s(s - 1)k + (s - 1)^2 k\u00b2 (1 + h\u00b2)\n\nCompare to right side:\n\n(1 + h\u00b2)(1 - s)^2 k\u00b2 + 4s(1 - s)k + 4s\u00b2\n\nNote that (1 - s)^2 = (s - 1)^2, so (1 + h\u00b2)(1 - s)^2 k\u00b2 = (1 + h\u00b2)(s - 1)^2 k\u00b2\n\nSimilarly, 4s(1 - s)k = -4s(s - 1)k\n\nSo right side becomes:\n\n(1 + h\u00b2)(s - 1)^2 k\u00b2 -4s(s - 1)k + 4s\u00b2\n\nComparing left and right:\n\nLeft: 4s\u00b2 - 4s(s - 1)k + (s - 1)^2 k\u00b2 (1 + h\u00b2 )\n\nRight: 4s\u00b2 -4s(s - 1)k + (s - 1)^2 k\u00b2 (1 + h\u00b2 )\n\nThey are identical. Therefore, left side equals right side, hence DE = QF.\n\nTherefore, after substituting the relation between r and k derived from the parallel conditions, both sides of the equation DE\u00b2 and QF\u00b2 are equal, hence DE = QF. Thus, the proof is complete.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{DE = QF}", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\( DE = QF \\) in the given isosceles triangle \\( ABC \\) with points \\( D \\) on \\( AB \\), \\( E \\) on \\( AC \\), and \\( F \\) on the extension of \\( BC \\) beyond \\( B \\) such that \\( CD = DF \\), and points \\( P \\) on line \\( DE \\) and \\( Q \\) on segment \\( BD \\) with \\( PF \\parallel AC \\) and \\( PQ \\parallel CD \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Coordinate Setup**:\n   - Place \\( B \\) at \\( (0, 0) \\), \\( C \\) at \\( (2, 0) \\), and \\( A \\) at \\( (1, h) \\).\n   - Parameterize points \\( D \\) and \\( E \\):\n     - \\( D \\) on \\( AB \\): \\( (1 - s, h(1 - s)) \\)\n     - \\( E \\) on \\( AC \\): \\( (1 + r, h(1 - r)) \\)\n   - Point \\( F \\) on the extension of \\( BC \\) beyond \\( B \\): \\( (-2s, 0) \\) (since \\( CD = DF \\)).\n\n2. **Coordinates of