5 = 0\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + (6/5)x + (12/5)y + 5/5 = 0\nSimplify:\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + (6/5)x + (12/5)y + 1 = 0\n\nTo eliminate fractions, multiply each term by 5:\n5x\u00b2 + 5y\u00b2 + 6x + 12y + 5 = 0\n\nLet me check if this is correct. Alternatively, maybe there's another approach. Let's recall that the equation of a circle with a given diameter can be written using the two endpoints. The general equation can be found by the equation: (x - x\u2081)(x - x\u2082) + (y - y\u2081)(y - y\u2082) = 0, where (x\u2081,y\u2081) and (x\u2082,y\u2082) are the endpoints of the diameter.\n\nSo, using the points (-1/5, -2/5) and (-1, -2):\n\n(x + 1/5)(x + 1) + (y + 2/5)(y + 2) = 0\n\nExpanding each term:\n\nFirst term: (x + 1/5)(x + 1) = x\u00b2 + x + (1/5)x + 1/5 = x\u00b2 + (6/5)x + 1/5\n\nSecond term: (y + 2/5)(y + 2) = y\u00b2 + 2y + (2/5)y + 4/5 = y\u00b2 + (12/5)y + 4/5\n\nAdding them together:\nx\u00b2 + (6/5)x + 1/5 + y\u00b2 + (12/5)y + 4/5 = 0\n\nCombine constants:\n1/5 + 4/5 = 5/5 = 1\n\nSo:\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + (6/5)x + (12/5)y + 1 = 0\n\nWhich is the same equation as before. So multiplying by 5 gives 5x\u00b2 + 5y\u00b2 + 6x + 12y + 5 = 0. Therefore, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, another approach: The desired circle should have the common chord as its diameter. Therefore, any point on the circle should satisfy the condition that the angle subtended by the common chord is a right angle (since the diameter subtends a right angle). But I think the methods above are sufficient.\n\nWait, let me cross-verify with another method. The radical axis of two circles is the line 2x - y = 0, which is the common chord. The center of the desired circle lies on the perpendicular bisector of the common chord. Wait, but the radical axis is the common chord, and the line joining the centers of C\u2081 and C\u2082 is perpendicular to the radical axis.\n\nBut perhaps this is complicating. Let me recall that the center of the desired circle must lie on the perpendicular bisector 