, b, or c, but not necessarily in the same order.\n\nSo, we have:\n\n{(a+b)(a+c), (a+b)(b+c), (a+c)(b+c)} = {a, b, c} (as sets)\n\nTherefore, each of the products must be equal to one of the original numbers. Let's denote the products as follows:\n\nLet P1 = (a+b)(a+c)\n\nP2 = (a+b)(b+c)\n\nP3 = (a+c)(b+c)\n\nSo, the set {P1, P2, P3} is equal to the set {a, b, c}.\n\nThis gives us a system of equations where each P is equal to one of a, b, c. But since the order is not specified, we need to consider all possible permutations.\n\nHowever, considering all permutations might be too tedious, so maybe there's a smarter way to approach this.\n\nFirst, let's note that all three products P1, P2, P3 are different unless some of a, b, c are equal. Similarly, the original numbers a, b, c might be all different or some equal.\n\nBut let's first assume that all three numbers a, b, c are distinct. Then, their sums will also be distinct, assuming a, b, c are distinct and positive. Wait, but if a, b, c are distinct, the sums could still be the same? For example, if a + b = a + c, then that would imply b = c, which would contradict them being distinct. Similarly, if a + b = b + c, that would imply a = c. So, if a, b, c are distinct, then all three sums in the second sequence are distinct. Then, the products P1, P2, P3 would be products of distinct pairs, so they might also be distinct. Therefore, the third sequence would also consist of three distinct numbers. Therefore, the original numbers a, b, c must be the same as these products, just in some order. So, each product must equal one of the original numbers.\n\nAlternatively, maybe two of the products are equal, but since a, b, c are distinct, that would require two of the original numbers to be equal, which contradicts the assumption. So perhaps all products are distinct, and each equals one of a, b, c.\n\nAlternatively, maybe two products equal to one number and the third equals another. But since there are three products and th