221a5 -1)/3 )\n\nSimilarly, point B = (b, -a) = ( 2(\u221a5 -1)/3 , 2(\u221a5 +1)/3 )\n\nNow, check if A is on the hyperbola x\u00b2 +2x -0.5y\u00b2 =0:\n\nCompute x\u00b2 +2x -0.5y\u00b2:\n\nx = -2(\u221a5 +1)/3, so x\u00b2 = [4(\u221a5 +1)^2]/9 = [4(6 + 2\u221a5)]/9 = (24 +8\u221a5)/9\n\n2x = -4(\u221a5 +1)/3\n\ny = 2(\u221a5 -1)/3, so y\u00b2 = [4(\u221a5 -1)^2]/9 = [4(6 - 2\u221a5)]/9 = (24 -8\u221a5)/9\n\n0.5 y\u00b2 = (12 -4\u221a5)/9\n\nThus, total:\n\n(24 +8\u221a5)/9 -4(\u221a5 +1)/3 - (12 -4\u221a5)/9\n\nConvert all terms to ninths:\n\n(24 +8\u221a5 -12\u221a5 -12 -12 +4\u221a5)/9\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n24 -12 -12 +8\u221a5 -12\u221a5 +4\u221a5 = 0 +0 =0\n\nHence, the equation holds. Similarly for point B.\n\nTherefore, these points are valid. Thus, for m=1/2, we have three distinct triangles:\n\n1. From Case 2a: A=(-4, -4), B=(-4,4)\n\n2. From Case 2b: A=( -2(\u221a5 +1)/3 , 2(\u221a5 -1)/3 ), B=( 2(\u221a5 -1)/3 , 2(\u221a5 +1)/3 )\n\n3. Another from the other t value (t=(3 -\u221a5)/2 \u22480.382), leading to another pair of points.\n\nFollowing similar calculations, the other solution would yield another valid pair of points. Hence, for m \u2208 (0,1), there are three distinct triangles.\n\nBut how many distinct triangles are there in total? For each m >0:\n\n- If m >1: Only one triangle (from Case 2a).\n\n- If m =1: No triangles.\n\n- If 0 < m <1: Three triangles (one from Case 2a, two from Case 2b).\n\nBut wait, when we derived Case 2b, we found two solutions for each m \u2208 (0,1). Hence, leading to two additional triangles, making total three.\n\nHowever, need to check if these two solutions from Case 2b are distinct from each other and from the Case 2a solution.\n\nIndeed, in the example with m=1/2, the two solutions from Case 2b are different from each other and from Case 2a. Hence, three distinct triangles.\n\nBut let's verify another value, say m=1/4.\n\nFrom Case 2a: a = 2/(1/4 -1) = 2/(-3/4) = -8/3. So A=(-8/3, -8/3), B=(-8/3, 8/3). Check