t each pair of quadratics intersects at exactly one point, implying they are tangent there.\n\n2. Express the differences between the quadratics as perfect squares with double roots at the intersection points.\n\n3. Show that equating these differences leads to a contradiction unless all double roots are the same.\n\n4. Conclude that the common intersection point must exist.\n\nTherefore, the proof is established.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that three quadratic polynomials with pairwise distinct leading coefficients, whose graphs pairwise intersect at exactly one common point, must all intersect at a single common point, follow this reasoning:\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation and Proof:**\n\n1. **Define the Quadratics:**\n   Let the three quadratics be:\n   \\[\n   f_1(x) = a_1x^2 + b_1x + c_1, \\quad\n   f_2(x) = a_2x^2 + b_2x + c_2, \\quad\n   f_3(x) = a_3x^2 + b_3x + c_3\n   \\]\n   with distinct leading coefficients \\(a_1, a_2, a_3\\).\n\n2. **Pairwise Intersection Condition:**\n   Each pair intersects at exactly one point, implying their difference is a perfect square:\n   \\[\n   f_i(x) - f_j(x) = (a_i - a_j)(x - x_{ij})^2 \\quad \\text{for } i \\neq j\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(x_{ij}\\) is the x-coordinate of the intersection point of \\(f_i\\) and \\(f_j\\).\n\n3. **Express Differences as Perfect Squares:**\n   Expanding the differences:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   f_1 - f_2 &= (a_1 - a_2)(x - x_{12})^2, \\\\\n   f_1 - f_3 &= (a_1 - a_3)(x - x_{13})^2, \\\\\n   f_2 - f_3 &= (a_2 - a_3)(x - x_{23})^2.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   These expansions yield relationships between coefficients:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   b_1 - b_2 &= -2(a_1 - a_2)x_{12}, \\\\\n   c_1 - c_2 &= (a_1 - a_2)x_{12}^2,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   and similarly for other pairs.\n\n4. **Equate X-Coordinates of Intersection Points:**\n   Assume the x-coordinates \\(x_{12}, x_{13}, x_{23}\\) are all equal to a common \\(x_0\\). Substitute into the coefficient equations:\n   \\[