D (which is at \u03b2 + 180\u00b0). The measure of \u2220BOD is the difference between these two angles.\n\nWait, angle between OB and OD. Since we are moving clockwise, the angle from OB to OD is \u03b2 + \u03b3. Wait, since OB to OC is \u03b2, and OC to OD is \u03b3, so total angle is \u03b2 + \u03b3.\n\nBut \u03b3 is 180\u00b0 - \u03b1, so \u2220BOD = \u03b2 + 180\u00b0 - \u03b1.\n\nBut we need to confirm that. Alternatively, since from OB to OD is the angle going through OC, which is \u03b2 + \u03b3. But since \u03b3 = 180\u00b0 - \u03b1, then \u2220BOD = \u03b2 + 180\u00b0 - \u03b1.\n\nTherefore, the bisector of \u2220BOD divides it into two angles of (\u03b2 + 180\u00b0 - \u03b1)/2 each.\n\nSo, the bisector of \u2220BOD is located at an angle of OB + (\u2220BOD)/2. Since OB is at angle \u03b1 from OA, then the bisector OF is at angle \u03b1 + (\u03b2 + 180\u00b0 - \u03b1)/2.\n\nSimplify that: \u03b1 + (\u03b2 + 180\u00b0 - \u03b1)/2 = (2\u03b1 + \u03b2 + 180\u00b0 - \u03b1)/2 = (\u03b1 + \u03b2 + 180\u00b0)/2.\n\nOn the other hand, the bisector OE of \u2220AOC is located at (\u03b1 + \u03b2)/2 from OA, since \u2220AOC = \u03b1 + \u03b2, so its bisector is halfway, which is (0 + \u03b1 + \u03b2)/2 = (\u03b1 + \u03b2)/2.\n\nTherefore, the angle of OE from OA is (\u03b1 + \u03b2)/2, and the angle of OF from OA is (\u03b1 + \u03b2 + 180\u00b0)/2.\n\nTo find the angle between OE and OF, we subtract their angles:\n\nAngle between OE and OF = |Angle of OF - Angle of OE| = |(\u03b1 + \u03b2 + 180\u00b0)/2 - (\u03b1 + \u03b2)/2| = |180\u00b0/2| = 90\u00b0.\n\nWait, that seems too straightforward. Let me check.\n\nOE is at (\u03b1 + \u03b2)/2.\n\nOF is at (\u03b1 + \u03b2 + 180\u00b0)/2.\n\nSo the difference between these two angles is [(\u03b1 + \u03b2 + 180\u00b0)/2] - [(\u03b1 + \u03b2)/2] = 180\u00b0/2 = 90\u00b0.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the values of \u03b1 and \u03b2, as long as the given condition \u03b1 + \u03b3 = 180\u00b0 holds, the angle between the bisec