ance, if \u03b1 is 180\u00b0, then OA and OB would be in opposite directions. But the problem states there are four distinct rays, so each angle between consecutive rays must be less than 180\u00b0. Therefore, \u03b1, \u03b2, \u03b3, \u03b4 are each between 0\u00b0 and 180\u00b0, and sum to 360\u00b0.\n\nGiven that, since \u03b1 + \u03b3 = 180\u00b0, and \u03b2 + \u03b4 = 180\u00b0, each of \u03b1 and \u03b3 must be less than 180\u00b0, so they are both positive angles adding to 180\u00b0, hence each between 0\u00b0 and 180\u00b0. Similarly, \u03b2 and \u03b4 are between 0\u00b0 and 180\u00b0.\n\nTherefore, \u2220BOD, which is \u03b2 + \u03b3, since \u03b2 and \u03b3 are both less than 180\u00b0, could be up to \u03b2 + \u03b3 = 180\u00b0 + \u03b2 (if \u03b3 is 180\u00b0, but \u03b3 can't be 180\u00b0 as \u03b1 would be 0\u00b0, which is not possible as rays are distinct). Wait, actually, since \u03b3 = 180\u00b0 - \u03b1, and \u03b1 > 0\u00b0, \u03b3 < 180\u00b0. Similarly, \u03b2 and \u03b4 are each less than 180\u00b0. Therefore, \u03b2 + \u03b3 = \u03b2 + 180\u00b0 - \u03b1.\n\nBut since \u03b1 and \u03b2 are both positive and less than 180\u00b0, \u03b2 + \u03b3 can range. For example, if \u03b1 is very small, say 10\u00b0, then \u03b3 = 170\u00b0, and \u03b2 can be up to 170\u00b0, so \u03b2 + \u03b3 can be up to 170\u00b0 + 170\u00b0 = 340\u00b0, which is more than 180\u00b0, but since \u03b2 and \u03b4 must sum to 180\u00b0, \u03b2 can't be more than 180\u00b0. Wait, no, if \u03b1 is 10\u00b0, then \u03b3 = 170\u00b0, and \u03b2 + \u03b4 = 180\u00b0, so \u03b2 can be up to 180\u00b0, but \u03b4 = 180\u00b0 - \u03b2 would be down to 0\u00b0, but \u03b4 has to be positive as rays are distinct, so \u03b2 < 180\u00b0, \u03b4 > 0\u00b0, hence \u03b2 < 180\u00b0, so \u03b2 + \u03b3 = \u03b2 + 170\u00b0, which could be up to 170\u00b0 + 180\u00b0 - \u03b5 \u2248 350\u00b0, but \u03b3 is 170\u00b0, so \u03b2 can be up to 180\u00b0 - \u03b5, so \u03b2 + \u03b3 can ap