ve Fibonacci numbers, it must satisfy \\( p(x+2) - p(x+1) - p(x) = 0 \\) for \\( x = 1, 2, \\ldots, 100 \\).\n\n2. **Constructing the Difference Polynomial**: Define \\( q(x) = p(x+2) - p(x+1) - p(x) \\). This polynomial \\( q(x) \\) has degree 100 and leading coefficient \\( -c_{100} \\), where \\( c_{100} \\) is the leading coefficient of \\( p(x) \\).\n\n3. **Roots of the Difference Polynomial**: Since \\( q(x) = 0 \\) for \\( x = 1, 2, \\ldots, 100 \\), it must be proportional to \\( (x - 1)(x - 2) \\cdots (x - 100) \\). Thus, \\( q(x) = -c_{100}(x - 1)(x - 2) \\cdots (x - 100) \\).\n\n4. **Evaluating at a Non-Root Point**: Consider evaluating \\( q(x) \\) at \\( x = 101 \\). This gives:\n   \\[\n   q(101) = p(103) - p(102) - p(101) = -c_{100} \\cdot 100!\n   \\]\n   However, since \\( p(103) \\) is not constrained by the problem, we use the 100th finite difference property.\n\n5. **Finite Difference Argument**: The 100th finite difference of a polynomial of degree 100 is \\( 100! \\) times its leading coefficient. For the Fibonacci sequence, the 100th finite difference at \\( x = 1 \\) is \\( a_1 = 1 \\). Therefore:\n   \\[\n   100! \\cdot c_{100} = 1 \\implies c_{100} = \\frac{1}{100!}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the leading coefficient of \\( p(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{100!}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo prove that if \\( p(x) \\) is a 100th-degree polynomial such that \\( p(1), p(2), \\ldots, p(102) \\) are consecutive elements of the Fibonacci sequence in order, then the leading coefficient of \\( p(x) \\) is \\( \\frac{1}{100!} \\), we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Define the Extended Fibonacci Sequence:**\n\n   Define the Fibonacci sequence for all integers (including non-positive integers), ensuring the recursion \\( a_{n+2} = a_{n+1} + a_{n} \\) holds for all \\( n \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   a_{0} & = a_{2} - a_{1} = 1 - 1 = 0 \\\\\n   a_{-1} & = a_{1} - a_{0} = 1 - 0 = 1 \\\\\n   a_{-2} & = a_{0} - a_{-1} = 0 - 1 = -1 \\\\\n   a_{-3} & = a_{-1} - a_{-2} = 1 - (-1) = 2\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Inductive Step Setup:**\n\n   Prove a more general statement: If \\( p \\) is an \\( n \\)-degree polynomial and \\( p(1), p(2), \\ldots, p(n+2) \\) are consecutive elements of the extended Fibonacci sequence, then the leading coefficient of \\( p \\) is \\( \\frac{1}{n!} \\).\n\n3. **Base Case:**\n\n   For \\( n = 0 \\) (constant polynomial):\n\n   \\( p(x) \\) is constant, so \\( p(1) = a_k \\) and \\( p(2) = a_{k+1} \\). Since \\( p \\) is constant, we have \\( a_k = a_{k+1} \\), implying \\( a_{k-1} = a_{k+1} - a_k = 0 \\). This happens only if \\( k = 1 \\) and thus \\( p(x) = a_1 = 1 \\). Therefore, the leading coefficient (constant term) is \\( 1 = \\frac{1}{0!} \\).\n\n4. **Inductive Hypothesis:**\n\n   Assume the general statement is true for \\( n = m-1 \\).\n\n5. **Inductive Step:**\n\n   For an \\( m \\)-degree polynomial \\( p \\) with \\( p(1) = a_k, p(2) = a_{k+1}, \\ldots, p(m+2) = a_{k+m+1} \\), write\n\n   \\[\n   p(x) = b_m x^m + b_{m-1} x^{m-1} + \\cdots + b_1 x + b_0\n   \\]\n\n   We need to show \\( b_m = \\frac{1}{m!} \\).\n\n6. **Construct Polynomial \\( q(x) \\):**\n\n   Define \\( q(x) = p(x+1) - p(x) \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   q(x) &= p(x+1) - p(x) \\\\\n   &= [b_m(x+1)^m + b_{m-1}(x+1)^{m-1} + \\cdots + b_1(x+1) + b_0] \\\\\n   &- [b_m x^m + b_{m-1} x^{m-1} + \\cdots + b_1 x + b_0]\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   After expanding and simplifying the polynomial,\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   q(x) &= \\left[ b_m \\left( \\sum_{i=0}^m \\binom{m}{i} x^{m-i} \\right) + b_{m-1} \\left( \\sum_{i=0}^{m-1} \\binom{m-1}{i} x^{m-1-i} \\right) + \\cdots + b_1 \\right] \\\\\n   &- \\left[ b_m x^m + b_{m-1} x^{m-1} + \\cdots + b_1 x \\right] \\\\\n   &\\approx m b_m x^{m-1} + \\text{lower order terms}\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   So, \\( q(x) \\) is an \\( (m-1) \\)-degree polynomial with leading coefficient \\( m b_m \\).\n\n7. **Inductive Hypothesis on \\( q(x) \